\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Multi-turn dialogue is the dominant mode of interaction with large language models (LLMs), and system prompts that specify task goals form the foundation of instruction following. As conversations grow longer, however, models progressively lose track of these goal specifications. \citet{dongre2026attention} quantified this degradation across 12 models: the Goal Attention Ratio (GAR), which measures how much attention the model allocates to goal tokens in the system prompt, declines by 27--48\% between early and mid-conversation turns. We replicate and extend this finding on Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct, observing a 70.8\% relative decline in GAR from 0.0418 at turn~1 to 0.0122 at mid-conversation. We attribute the steeper decline partly to our use of last-token GAR, which captures summary-position attention more sensitively than the all-token average of \citet{dongre2026attention}, and partly to Qwen-2.5-7B's stronger instruction-following fidelity in early turns. This attention decay represents a systematic failure of multi-turn models to maintain task coherence, and it grows worse with each additional turn.

A striking aspect of this degradation is what it does \emph{not} affect. \citet{dongre2026attention} showed that goal-relevant information persists in the residual stream throughout the conversation: linear discriminant analysis probes recover goal content with AUC\,=\,0.98 even at late turns where attention to goal tokens has collapsed. The information is present in the hidden states, but the attention mechanism fails to route it toward the output computation. This dissociation between information availability and information utilization suggests a natural intervention: if the residual stream already carries goal information, a learnable gate on the residual pathway could amplify or attenuate layer contributions to recover goal attention. This reasoning motivates ThreadGate, which modulates each residual update with a content-and-turn-conditioned MLP gate: $x_{l+1} = x_l + g_l \cdot f_l(x_l)$, where $g_l$ is produced by a two-layer network conditioned on the hidden state and turn features, inspired by the residual scaling idea of ReZero \citep{bachlechner2021rezero}.

\begin{figure*}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig1_overview.pdf}
  \caption{Overview of ThreadGate and its failure mode. (Left) ThreadGate adds per-layer content-and-turn-conditioned MLP gates to the residual stream, trained jointly with an auxiliary GAR loss that targets goal attention. (Right) Under teacher-forced evaluation, GAR-ours reaches 0.50 across all fine-tuned variants; under autoregressive generation, it drops to 0.015--0.025, a 20--33$\times$ gap. The five-way comparison (ThreadGate, +PCGrad, Uniform Gate, LoRA-only, Base) shows that no intervention provides measurable benefit over LoRA-only fine-tuning.}
  \label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}

ThreadGate combines per-layer MLP gate networks with a joint training objective: next-token prediction (NTP), an auxiliary GAR loss that pushes attention toward goal tokens via an MSE target ($\tau = 0.5$), and a gate diversity loss that prevents all gates from collapsing to a single value (\S\ref{sec:method}). The final loss formulation emerged from seven design iterations (v1 through v7) spanning four loss families (cumulative, maximize, smooth saturation, and MSE target); only the MSE target with gate diversity converged stably. To diagnose why the auxiliary objective fails under generation, we construct a causal diagnostic framework built on a five-way factorial comparison: ThreadGate (turn-conditioned gates), +PCGrad (ThreadGate with gradient conflict elimination), Uniform Gate (content-only gates), LoRA-only (gates fixed at 1.0), and the unmodified Base model. Each pair targets one primary causal factor (gradient conflict, turn conditioning, gating mechanism, or fine-tuning) and produces four targeted causal contrasts (\S\ref{sec:experiments}). PCGrad \citep{yu2020pcgrad} serves a dual role in this framework: beyond a training strategy, it functions as a causal exclusion tool that rules out gradient direction conflict as a candidate explanation.

Training succeeds: teacher-forced GAR-ours reaches 0.50 across all fine-tuned variants. The factorial comparison reveals that the failure is specific to autoregressive generation, where GAR-ours drops to 0.015--0.025, a 20--33$\times$ gap that persists across all training strategies. All four fine-tuned variants achieve indistinguishable performance (0.75\,pp spread on GAR-ours, identical PPL at 2.78); the gating contrast confirms that gates contribute zero measurable benefit. The PCGrad causal exclusion eliminates gradient direction conflict without altering any outcome, establishing that conflict is neither necessary nor sufficient for the failure. These four contrasts converge on a single root cause: exposure bias \citep{bengio2015scheduled}, where attention patterns optimized under teacher-forced prefixes do not transfer to model-generated text (Figure~\ref{fig:overview}).

Our contributions: (1)~a systematic causal diagnostic framework for analyzing auxiliary objective failures in multi-turn dialogue, comprising a five-way factorial comparison that isolates four causal factors (turn conditioning, gating mechanism, gradient conflict, and fine-tuning), PCGrad-based causal exclusion via gradient surgery \citep{yu2020pcgrad}, and per-layer gradient conflict quantification; this design pattern is in principle applicable to other auxiliary objective analyses (\S\ref{sec:method}, \S\ref{sec:experiments}, \S\ref{sec:analysis}); (2)~using this framework, the first empirical characterization of how exposure bias selectively destroys auxiliary attention loss generalization (20--33$\times$ gap) while leaving NTP robust, with evidence that token-level self-recovery \citep{he2019exposure} does not extend to auxiliary objectives; (3)~a principled negative baseline for future attention steering research: ThreadGate with seven loss formulations and four training configurations ruled out (\S\ref{sec:method}, \S\ref{sec:experiments}).

\subsection{Inference-Time Attention Steering}\label{subsec:steering}

The preceding sections established that all training-time interventions fail uniformly: exposure bias collapses autoregressive GAR regardless of gating or gradient management. We now test whether inference-time attention manipulation can recover GAR without retraining. We apply an additive bias $\beta$ to goal-token attention logits at every layer of the v7 ThreadGate checkpoint during generation.

Figure~\ref{fig:steering} summarizes both evaluation protocols. Under autoregressive generation ($N$\,=\,5), low bias values ($\beta \in [0, 2]$) yield negligible GAR change (0.028\,$\to$\,0.062) with stable PPL (${\sim}$1.4). A phase transition emerges between $\beta$\,=\,2 and $\beta$\,=\,5: GAR jumps from 0.062 to 0.680 while PPL rises only to 1.91. At $\beta$\,=\,5, generation GAR (0.680) surpasses the unsteered teacher-forced baseline (0.535). Further increase to $\beta$\,=\,10 yields GAR\,=\,0.959 but degrades PPL to 4.22. Teacher-forced evaluation ($N$\,=\,200) confirms steering effectiveness, though PPL cost escalates sharply ($\beta$\,=\,10: PPL\,=\,505).

These results close the causal loop identified in \S\ref{sec:analysis}: exposure bias shifts attention away from goal tokens, GAR collapses, and manually correcting attention via $\beta$ bias restores it. The failure resides at the attention routing layer, and inference-time intervention circumvents exposure bias. Two caveats apply: generation evaluation uses only $N$\,=\,5 conversations due to compute constraints, so the precise phase-transition boundary requires finer $\beta$ sweeps. Steering trades fluency for goal recall; practical deployment would need a Pareto-optimal $\beta$.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig5_attention_steering.pdf}
  \caption{Inference-time attention steering: additive bias $\beta$ applied to goal-token attention logits. (a)~Under autoregressive generation ($N$\,=\,5), a phase transition occurs at $\beta \in [2, 5]$: GAR jumps from 0.062 to 0.680 with modest PPL cost (1.41$\to$1.91). At $\beta$\,=\,5, generation GAR (0.680) exceeds the unsteered teacher-forced baseline (0.535, dotted line). (b)~Under teacher forcing ($N$\,=\,200), steering is effective but PPL cost escalates sharply ($\beta$\,=\,10: PPL\,=\,505).}
  \label{fig:steering}
\end{figure}

